# Secret. Only for the `restore-check` ops job: ${SF_SECRETS_DIR}/restore-test.env. A DISPOSABLE database that
# is never the staging database; its contents are overwritten. The script refuses a target with the same host,
# port and database name as DATABASE_URL (backup.env), a target whose database name does not contain "restore",
# "test" or "scratch", and a target that resolves to the same server and database (identity check). URL shape: see
# migrate.env.example (query limited to sslmode, sslrootcert, connect_timeout; database name A-Za-z0-9_.-).
# Percent-encode reserved characters in USER and PASSWORD.
#
# Who is this role. The restore WRITES to the target, so it must be a role that owns the scratch database and that
# has NO access to the live one. It is not force_backup (read-only, live side) and not force_migrator (owner of
# live objects). The scratch database and its role are created ONCE by the database administrator, outside this
# job: restore-check never creates a database and force_backup has no CREATEDB. Typical shapes:
#   - a separate scratch server (or managed instance): any role that owns the scratch database there;
#   - the same server: CREATE ROLE force_restore LOGIN PASSWORD '...'; CREATE DATABASE sf_restore_test OWNER
#     force_restore; REVOKE ALL ON DATABASE sf_restore_test FROM PUBLIC; (force_restore needs no CREATEDB to restore
#     into a database it already owns; extensions the dump needs, such as pg_trgm, are trusted extensions).
RESTORE_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://REPLACE_USER:REPLACE_PASSWORD@REPLACE_HOST:5432/REPLACE_RESTORE_TEST_DB?sslmode=verify-full
# With sslmode=verify-full against a managed database, add &sslrootcert=<mounted CA file path> and mount that
# file read-only in the restore-check container (see migrate.env.example).
